WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.790
Film Courage: You know we talked about this
earlier and I hope you’re okay talking about

00:00:02.790 --> 00:00:03.790
this again?

00:00:03.790 --> 00:00:04.790
Larry Wilson: Yes.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:10.460
Film Courage: Okay...about not going to college
or going to junior college and not finishing

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:16.740
[a 2-year degree] and still having confidence
and being able to believe in yourself even

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.880
if somebody in an executive’s suit sitting
on the other side of this really nice Mahogany

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.290
desk with their arms folded tells you you’re
not okay.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.860
But also knowing that you are [okay].

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.469
Where does that come from, because I don’t
think that is something innate.

00:00:34.469 --> 00:00:38.980
You probably develop it over time, wouldn’t
you say?

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.870
Larry Wilson: Yeah...and it was an issue for
me...there is no doubt about it.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:53.510
When I became a studio executive at Paramount
working most closely with Jeff Katzenberg.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:01:01.010
All of the sudden there was this (as these
bizarre trends happened and you don’t quite

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:07.630
understand where they come from and then would
go away), but all of the sudden it became

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:18.810
very important to hire people in development
positions who had a Harvard education and

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:27.600
I was working with a couple of them and they
also went to the school of Machiavelli I guess

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:36.430
and had read and reread The Art of War and
strategically on that level they could take

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.090
me out, okay.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:48.009
But I thought about it and I thought I’ve
loved movies since I can remember.

00:01:48.009 --> 00:01:55.159
And while I was terrible in school (I was
a terrible student), my mom loved reading.

00:01:55.159 --> 00:01:58.999
She taught me the love of reading, so I had
that on my side.

00:01:58.999 --> 00:02:08.850
But what I really had on my side was if I
loved a movie, I LOVED a movie, I loved it!

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:16.940
And I realized pretty quickly that “the
movies,” the movie business, it was not

00:02:16.940 --> 00:02:21.940
a business meant for Harvard grads.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:28.820
And I could read a script and say “This
is a movie” and say it with as much confidence

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:35.690
as possible and mean it and believe it.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:42.670
But I was up against this...and my own inferiority
complex sometimes...about not having been

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:46.090
a good student and having barely gotten out
of high school.

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.990
It would work on me sometimes.

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:55.990
But one day after I had been completely creamed
in a development war with one of these Harvard

00:02:55.990 --> 00:03:03.170
grads, Jeff Katzenberg took me aside and he
said “Larry, you need to learn to be a straight

00:03:03.170 --> 00:03:05.320
player in a crooked game.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:10.240
I’m not here for you to out-manipulate anyone.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:18.060
You’re sitting with me and you’re sitting
beside me here because when you love something,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.450
you love it.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:25.340
And when you read a script and you love it,
you’re going to make sure that everyone

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.830
else loves it, too.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.370
And that’s what I like in you.

00:03:30.370 --> 00:03:32.750
And that’s why you’re valuable to me.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:36.200
Stop trying to be a studio executive.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:42.330
Be a guy who can read a script or watch a
movie and get excited the way you get excited.”

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:54.870
And that was a huge shot in the arm of confidence
that I took away.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:02.320
I owe Jeff Katzenberg for a lot of things
that he wouldn’t even be aware of.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:08.120
But that was a big one because he told me
“Just be yourself and use this passion for

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:12.020
movies and this love for movies and make that
your calling card.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.880
Don’t try to be ‘A Player.’

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.220
You’re not good at it.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:20.380
You’re horrible at it.”

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:25.070
Which I am, because I wear everything on my
sleeve.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.160
Ask my wife, yeah...

